<spell-notebook "quote usage">
	<header level="1">
		Quote
		<header>A quote holds someone else's words:  a block of its own, or a few words inside a sentence.</>
	</>
	<quote-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-quote>` holds someone else's words.

			- By default it's a block quote:  its own block, a bar on its leading edge, the text muted.
			- `inline` puts it inside a sentence instead, with quotation marks round it.
			  The marks are the browser's for the text's language:  “…” in English, « … » in French.
			- `cite` is the address the words come from.  It isn't shown:  it's for readers' tools.
			- The look is the Markdown pages' block quote.  Its tokens, `--ui-quote-color` and `--ui-quote-border`,
			  fall back to the Markdown ones, so a theme that sets those sets both.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			A block quote is a real `<blockquote>`, and an inline one a real `<q>`:  screen readers announce them as quotations.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
